@title(The imaging studio: CT in the document, de-identified in the browser)
@author(IDoc craft demos, November 2026)

@abstract
The dicom block puts a medical imaging viewer in the document. The engine is IDoc's own, written in Rust and compiled to WebAssembly: it decodes DICOM, de-identifies it, stores it as a compact block beside the document, draws it, and grades questions about it. Every study on this page is synthetic. There is no patient data anywhere in this folder.
@end

@section(A first study)

The built-in phantom is a synthetic chest CT made by the engine itself, sixty slices of 192 by 192 pixels. The block's body is the view: here, two panes side by side, the same slice in a lung window and a soft-tissue window.

@dicom(src=phantom)
layout 1x2
series 2 window=lung slice=31
series 2 window=soft slice=31
@end

Scroll in a pane or drag the slider to move through the slices; drag with the window tool to change the window width and level. Every change writes the view back as a line, so the text in the editor always says what the reader is looking at. The toolbar also has measurement tools (length, angle, region), the multiplanar planes and a 3D view.

@section(De-identification, shown on fake patient data)

The sidecar holds synthetic-ct-with-fake-phi.zip: the same phantom written as 24 ordinary DICOM files by the engine's own test writer, with made-up identifying data in the headers: a patient name (PHANTOM^Chest), a patient ID (PH-0001), a birth date, an accession number, a referring physician, an institution (Phantom General Hospital), a device serial number and a private vendor tag. The engine's PHI check, run on one of those raw files, names eleven tags that must not leave the machine: (0008,0050), (0008,0080), (0008,0090), (0008,1010), the private (0009,0010) and (0009,1001), (0010,0010), (0010,0020), (0010,0030), (0018,1000) and (0020,0010).

This block names the zip. The card unpacks it in this browser, de-identifies every file by the DICOM standard's basic profile (PS3.15 Annex E), and only then builds the block it draws.

@dicom(src=07-dicom.assets/synthetic-ct-with-fake-phi.zip)
@end

Look at the top left corner of the image, where a viewer prints the patient's name: it reads ANON. Names, dates and identifiers are gone or replaced, UIDs are remapped consistently so the series still hangs together, and the private tags are dropped. The first time the block opens, the card saves the de-identified study beside the document as a .bvol block and rewrites the block's head to name it, adding a study line that records its size and spacing; from then on the zip is not needed. The server checks again: it refuses to store a block that still names a patient. A person's own CD can be opened on their own machine with nothing uploaded at all.

@section(An image as a question)

The block is the question. The student is asked to mark the nodule; the key is the point in the solution section, which the student never receives. hotspot(lesion)=solution ±8mm gives the points when the student's mark lands within 8 millimetres of the key's, measured in the patient's own coordinates, and slice=solution ±5 checks they found the right level.

@dicom(src=phantom, id=nodule, pts=10, expect="hotspot(lesion)=solution ±8mm, slice=solution ±5")
series 2 window=lung slice=20
@solution
series 2 slice 31 point 58,78 label=lesion
@end

The other things a question can ask for include a length or an angle measured within a tolerance, a region compared with the key by its overlap, and a written report compared with the key's findings and impression.
